	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/08/43773</id><title>For Brazil’s Indigenous youth, visibility is resistance</title><updated>2026-08-08T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/08/43773" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/05-08-26-ONUNews-BrazilIndigenous01.png/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/05-08-26-ONUNews-BrazilIndigenous01.png/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/05-08-26-ONUNews-BrazilIndigenous01.png/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/05-08-26-ONUNews-BrazilIndigenous01.png/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As climate change reshapes Indigenous territories across Brazil, a new generation is turning to culture and digital platforms to defend their lands and their future. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/08/43773&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “For Brazil’s Indigenous youth, visibility is resistance”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43767</id><title>In Taliban-Ruled Afghanistan, One Woman Refuses a Life of Dependence</title><updated>2026-08-07T17:13:14-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43767" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/afghanwomenworking.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/afghanwomenworking.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/afghanwomenworking-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/afghanwomenworking.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
KABUL, August 7 (IPS)  - The Taliban’s rise to power robbed Mehrmaa of her husband, her job and her chance to live freely.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43767&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “In Taliban-Ruled Afghanistan, One Woman Refuses a Life of Dependence”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43766</id><title>Hiroshima’s Question: Will We Continue to Live Under the Nuclear Shadow?</title><updated>2026-08-07T07:08:24-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43766" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Hiroshima_1.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Hiroshima_1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Hiroshima_1-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Hiroshima_1.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
HIROSHIMA, Japan, August 7 (IPS)  - This year’s Hiroshima Peace Declaration did not begin with words of remembrance alone. It opened instead with what the declaration called “military invasions using nuclear weapons as tools of intimidation,” “imperious actions by major powers in violation of international law,” and the failure of the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, or NPT, Review Conference — for the third consecutive time — to adopt a final document.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43766&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Hiroshima’s Question: Will We Continue to Live Under the Nuclear Shadow?”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43762</id><title>Burkina Faso’s Cancelled Democracy</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:58:03-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43762" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Burkina-Fasos-junta_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Burkina-Fasos-junta_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Burkina-Fasos-junta_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Burkina-Fasos-junta_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
LONDON, August 7 (IPS)  - Burkina Faso’s charismatic young military leader, army captain Ibrahim Traoré, has stopped pretending. In April, he &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/03/people-burkina-faso-should-forget-about-democracy-military-ruler-ibrahim-traore&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;declared&lt;/a&gt; that people should ‘forget about the question of democracy’ because ‘democracy isn’t for us’. When Traoré seized power in September 2022, ousting a leader who’d staged a coup earlier that year, he promised elections by June 2024. The junta broke that pledge, pushing any transition back to 2029. Now Traoré has confirmed he has no intention of restoring democracy. Instead, Burkina Faso’s military junta is tightening its repression, while working with neighbouring states to internationalise its dangerously undemocratic model.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43762&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Burkina Faso’s Cancelled Democracy”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43761</id><title>The World’s Aid Workers Must Be Protected</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:16:54-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43761" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Action-Against-Hunger_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Action-Against-Hunger_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Action-Against-Hunger_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Action-Against-Hunger_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NAIROBI, Kenya, August 7 (IPS)  - This summer, an ambush on a marked humanitarian convoy in Duk County, South Sudan, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.actionagainsthunger.org/press-releases/action-against-hunger-condemns-deadly-attack-on-aid-convoy-carrying-partner-personnel-in-south-sudan-demands-safety-for-humanitarian-workers/&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;left 17 dead and five injured&lt;/a&gt;. While the &lt;a href=&quot;https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/06/1167847&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;UN called for an immediate investigation&lt;/a&gt;, history suggests the victims’ families &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.actionagainsthunger.org/app/uploads/2026/07/Rapport-Muttur-2026_EN_web.pdf&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;will wait indefinitely for answers&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43761&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The World’s Aid Workers Must Be Protected”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43772</id><title>The Mediterranean’s own odyssey: An ecological treasure feels the strain</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43772" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/05-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Temple-Apollo.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/05-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Temple-Apollo.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/05-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Temple-Apollo.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/05-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Temple-Apollo.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;More than 2,500 years after Homer’s epic was first told, Christopher Nolan&#039;s blockbuster retelling of The Odyssey has brought the journey back to big screens worldwide. But, behind the sweeping plot is another character facing an odyssey of its own: the Mediterranean Sea.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43772&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “The Mediterranean’s own odyssey: An ecological treasure feels the strain”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43771</id><title>‘Sudan is losing another generation’ to war, Mohammed warns</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43771" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/06/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/06/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/06/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/06/12-11-2025-UNICEF-Sudan-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;More than 1,000 days of war in Sudan have left millions of children out of school, displaced or exposed to grave violations, the UN Deputy Secretary-General said on Friday, calling for renewed action to end the conflict. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43771&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Sudan is losing another generation’ to war, Mohammed warns”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43770</id><title>Peacock party takes pride of place in nod to decades of turbulent history</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43770" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-UN-Photo-peacocks-01.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-UN-Photo-peacocks-01.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-UN-Photo-peacocks-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-UN-Photo-peacocks-01.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In a new twist on panda diplomacy, UN Geneva welcomed five young peacocks to the Palace of Nations on Friday, a gift of India that preserves a tradition of keeping the stately birds in the grounds, that predates both the global body and its ill-fated predecessor, the League of Nations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43770&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Peacock party takes pride of place in nod to decades of turbulent history”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43769</id><title>Ebola in DR Congo: Childhood deaths rise; hopes raised over new vaccine</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43769" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/06/19-06-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/06/19-06-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/06/19-06-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/06/19-06-2026-WHO-DR-Congo-ebola-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;More than 300 children have died in the Ebola outbreak in the eastern Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), the UN said on Friday, as health experts urged trials of the only vaccine against the disease to halt further spread of the rare Bundibugyo species.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43769&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Ebola in DR Congo: Childhood deaths rise; hopes raised over new vaccine”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43768</id><title>Food prices edged up in July amid heatwaves and energy price hikes</title><updated>2026-08-07T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43768" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-Brazil-sugarcane-Adobe.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-Brazil-sugarcane-Adobe.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-Brazil-sugarcane-Adobe.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/07-08-2026-Brazil-sugarcane-Adobe.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The cost of key foods edged up last month as searing heatwaves and ongoing geopolitical uncertainty impacted energy prices around the world.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/07/43768&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Food prices edged up in July amid heatwaves and energy price hikes”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0011s ch:1 -->